Give ResolvedCursorInfo a virtual destructor.

ResolvedCursorInfo is the base of a hierarchy (ResolvedValueRefCursorInfo,
ResolvedModuleRefCursorInfo, ResolvedExprStartCursorInfo,
ResolvedStmtStartCursorInfo) whose objects are made with new in
CursorInfoResolver and owned as ResolvedCursorInfoPtr, an
llvm::IntrusiveRefCntPtr<ResolvedCursorInfo>. Its refcount is
llvm::RefCountedBase<ResolvedCursorInfo>, whose Release() does
`delete static_cast<const ResolvedCursorInfo *>(this)`. Without a virtual
destructor that deletes a subclass through the base: undefined behaviour,
the subclass's members (ResolvedValueRefCursorInfo's two SmallVectors) are
never destroyed, and the sized operator delete is passed the base's size,
not the 232 bytes that were allocated. Allocators that use the size
(tcmalloc, jemalloc, mimalloc with sized deallocation) can put the block back
into the wrong size class.

Symptom: sourcekit-lsp-lite's asan preset, run against the official
Swift 6.3.3 libsourcekitdInProc.so, aborts in the rename test with
"AddressSanitizer: new-delete-type-mismatch", allocated in
CursorInfoResolver::tryResolve and freed from ~ASTUnit as the cached
CursorInfoRequest results go (the evaluator's
PerRequestCache::makeEmpty<CursorInfoRequest> deleter). The 6.4 release's
library does the same. A reduced copy of the hierarchy under
-fsanitize=address reports the same mismatch, and runs clean with the
destructor virtual.

The fix is the one SourceKit's own ref-counted hierarchy (ImmutableTextUpdate)
and LLVM's (vfs::FileSystem over ThreadSafeRefCountedBase) already use.
Nothing relies on the base being non-polymorphic: every instance is made with
new and reached through a pointer, kinds are told apart by CursorInfoKind and
classof, which a vtable does not change, and no instance is copied, stored by
value or memcpy'd. The non-atomic refcount is not a second bug: every retain
and release happens on the owning AST's serial consumer queue or while that
AST is destroyed.

unittests/IDE/ResolvedCursorInfoTest.cpp is the regression test for the
upstream PR (this build does not compile unittests): a subclass released
through ResolvedCursorInfoPtr must have its destructor run, which fails
without the fix on any build; a second case fails only under ASan or LSan.
docs/upstream-todo-resolvedcursorinfo.md has the history and the draft PR.

Upstream: the same on main (include/swift/IDE/Utils.h, from PR #63612,
"Pass ResolvedCursorInfo as shared pointer instead of by value", which
made it a class hierarchy); not reported.

diff --git a/include/swift/IDE/Utils.h b/include/swift/IDE/Utils.h
index e7ff7fb..02bd0a5 100644
--- a/include/swift/IDE/Utils.h
+++ b/include/swift/IDE/Utils.h
@@ -149,6 +149,11 @@ public:
   ResolvedCursorInfo() = default;
   ResolvedCursorInfo(SourceFile *SF) : SF(SF) {}
 
+  /// Virtual because the subclasses are owned as \c ResolvedCursorInfoPtr,
+  /// whose \c llvm::RefCountedBase<ResolvedCursorInfo> deletes them through a
+  /// \c ResolvedCursorInfo pointer when the last reference goes.
+  virtual ~ResolvedCursorInfo() = default;
+
   CursorInfoKind getKind() const { return Kind; }
 
   SourceFile *getSourceFile() const { return SF; }
